# luxe

Root invariants for the luxe codebase. Picked up by `find_all_sdd` and
surfaced to the model in the task prompt when `set_repo_root` points at
the luxe checkout itself; read by humans and Claude Code as the
authoritative architectural contract.

Sourced from `lessons.md` postmortems and the durable rules in user
auto-memory.

## Must
- Mono-only execution mode (single capable model, full tool surface)
- temp=0.0 in production fixture configs
- Pin `Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-6bit` as the single champion MoE model (configured in
  `configs/single_64gb.yaml`); the m5max_moe bake-off (2026-05-10) is settled
- No per-task model selection, model fan-out, or router; re-bench only on
  explicit user request and write results under `acceptance/m5max_moe_<id>/`.
  Sole sanctioned exceptions, BOTH scoped to the interactive front-end
  (`luxe chat` / `luxe code`) and never the benchmark/maintain path:
  (a) `luxe chat` slots (chat/plan/code), opt-in via `configs/chat.yaml`
  `slots:`; (b) per-HOST manifests (`hosts:` in chat.yaml, 2026-07-30
  fallback-kit pivot) — each fleet host declares an interactive main +
  fallback pair sized to its RAM, with loud auto-degrade (chat.sdd). The
  champion pin above is a BENCHMARK pin: `single_64gb.yaml` still selects it
  and m1 keeps its weights (manifest `keep:`), but the interactive default on
  m1/m4 is deliberately NOT the champion. A host with no `hosts:` entry is
  champion-everywhere, byte-identical to the pre-manifest behaviour
- Multi-backend endpoint selection (`backends:` map + `/backend`/`--backend`)
  joins the slots carve-out: chat-only, config-sourced (BackendEntry =
  base_url/api_key_env/timeout_s/stall_timeout_s/decode_stall_timeout_s/
  shared/default; keys resolve from env at Backend construction, NEVER from
  YAML). The two
  stall bounds are the B6 PROGRESS deadlines and default to `None` = "use
  Backend's default", so an entry that omits them is byte-identical to one
  written before they existed; the numbers live in `backend.py` alone.
  `shared` (auto-detected: non-loopback = shared) turns OFF single-residency
  eviction — luxe frees only models THIS session loaded, never another
  client's (B5).
  When `backends:` is absent a single "local" entry is synthesized from
  `omlx_base_url` so existing configs parse identically; benchmark/maintain
  keep reading `omlx_base_url` only
- Backend retry decisions log on BOTH response paths (2026-08-04):
  `_chat_stream` mirrors the non-stream path's
  `logger.warning("backend … decision=…")` lines so an outage's retry
  history reaches any installed session log handler (chat debug.log — the
  scenario the fallback kit exists for). `on_retry` stays callback-only;
  never remove the log lines in favor of it, and keep the two paths' line
  format identical (one parser in `scripts/toolcall_taxonomy.py` covers both)
- Payload-suspect annotation (pinned 2026-08-24, opt-in, OBSERVE-ONLY,
  UNBENCHED). `LUXE_PAYLOAD_SUSPECT_RETRY=1` (opt-IN grammar — ONLY the exact
  string "1", `agents/flags.py`'s spelling for an unbenched lever; read at
  call time, never cached) makes `classify_failure` SUFFIX its reason with
  `-payload-suspect` when a transport-level failure follows a prompt that both
  (a) exceeds `_PAYLOAD_SUSPECT_MIN_CHARS` (65,536 prompt chars ≈ 16k tokens,
  half the smallest window luxe fields) and (b) is ≥ `_PAYLOAD_SUSPECT_GROWTH`
  (2.0×) the last request THIS endpoint accepted. **It changes no decision.**
  Retry, delay, and every branch — 4xx, 429, 5xx-transient, 5xx-terminal,
  warmup, exhausted-attempts — are byte-identical in both arms; with the lever
  off nothing is even measured (`prompt_chars` is not called and
  `Backend._last_accepted_prompt_chars` stays None). Pinned by
  `tests/test_backend_retry.py::TestEveryExistingDecisionIsUnchanged`, which
  replays the whole decision table in both arms with growth-shaped sizes
  attached.
  - Why observe-only rather than the "at most one retry" the plan proposes
    (`acceptance/chat_bigread_2026_08_24/PLAN.md` 3.1, which authorises this
    call): the evidence is ONE session, ONE provider, ONE exception class
    (`168f1825a1fd`, RemoteProtocolError on openrouter), and REPORT.md's A/B
    matrix is still PENDING. More decisive: >2× growth over the last accepted
    request is the ORDINARY shape of an early agent turn — step 1 is
    system-prompt-and-tool-JSON, step 2 carries the first tool result — so the
    heuristic's false-positive population is "any turn that read a file and
    then hit a network blip", which is precisely the outage this tool exists
    to survive. Cutting the ladder from 3 to 1 there is a worse outcome than
    the 81.5s of waste it would save.
  - The state lives on the `Backend` INSTANCE
    (`_last_accepted_prompt_chars`, updated only on a request the server
    answered) because "the last request this endpoint took" is a property of
    the endpoint, and because `agents/loop.py`'s `backend.chat` call site and
    `run_agent`'s signature are frozen (chat.sdd Must-not) — a parameter could
    never be threaded from the path where the failure happens. Same precedent
    as `on_reasoning`/`engine_label`. `classify_failure` itself stays a PURE
    keyword-only function: the two sizes are optional args, defaulting to
    None = "not measured", so every direct unit call is unaffected.
  - Annotation rides `reason`, never a new `RetryDecision` field:
    `scripts/bigread_drill.py:241` parses the dataclass REPR out of debug.log,
    and a new field would change every decision's repr on every path including
    with the lever off. The `transient-<ExcName>` prefix is preserved.
  - Transport-level failures ONLY. A status-carrying failure was ingested and
    answered — 4xx (`context_length_exceeded` included) already fails fast and
    5xx already has a body to classify; a size guess could only add noise.
    A first-ever request with no accepted baseline is never suspicious: the
    claim is about GROWTH, and there is none to measure.
  - **UNBENCHED.** Promotion — to the enforcing form (cap the ladder at one
    retry, then let the caller shrink) — needs a corpus from this lever: the
    bar is the taxonomy bar (≥5 occurrences across ≥2 distinct sessions,
    `scripts/toolcall_taxonomy.py`) PLUS the false-positive count, i.e. how
    often `-payload-suspect` appears on a retry that then SUCCEEDED. A lever
    that annotates turns which go on to recover is a lever that would have
    broken them. Enforcement is also a benchmark-path change (the loop retries
    on this classification) and needs maintain_suite 3 reps × 10
- Vendor request fields go at the TOP LEVEL of the chat body, never nested
  under `extra_body` (2026-08-11). `extra_body` is an OpenAI **SDK**
  convention — the SDK flattens that dict before sending. `backend.chat`
  posts raw JSON (`json=body`), so nesting emitted a literal
  `{"extra_body": {...}}` field that no server reads: `num_ctx` and
  `repeat_penalty` were both dropped on the wire for the life of the file,
  and it is why C10 measured a "no-op" (RESUME.md, retracted). Flattening is
  safe because servers ignore unknown top-level fields (oMLX's
  `ChatCompletionRequest` declares no `extra` policy → pydantic v2 defaults
  to "ignore"). `repeat_penalty` is sent under BOTH spellings — llama.cpp
  wants `repeat_penalty`, oMLX wants `repetition_penalty` — since the fleet
  runs both and each ignores the other's. Pinned by
  `tests/test_backend_vendor_fields.py` + the golden request snapshot.
  Corollary: `num_ctx` reaching the server is NOT evidence the window was
  negotiated — oMLX has no per-request context knob at any spelling and
  enforces the model's native length with a 400
- All mono prompt edits go through `src/luxe/agents/prompts.py` registry
- Pin `--work-dir ~/.luxe/bench-workspace` for bench runs (random tempdir leaks into prompts)
- Fetch model weights through oMLX's own downloader (`/admin/api/hf/*`, wrapped by
  `modelstore.OmlxAdmin`) — never a second `snapshot_download`, which would race
  the server over the same HF cache. Mount imports are plain copies into
  `~/.omlx/models/`, resolving symlinks AND Synology `XSym` stubs
- `oMLX` `idle_timeout_seconds: 1800` (null keeps models resident forever)
- `--keep-loaded` for bench mode (post-run unload fires by default)
- The anti-fumble surfaces are POINT-IN-TIME and USER-INVOKED (2026-08-04):
  `luxe ready` (alias `luxe doctor`) answers "can I work right now?" in
  seconds with no model — it reuses `/doctor`'s checks verbatim against a
  stand-in session and prints them through the SHARED renderer
  (`chat.inspection.render_doctor`), so the in-session and host-level tables
  can never drift; `luxe outage` / `/outage` print `OUTAGE.md`, the offline
  emergency card, through one reader (`luxe.outage.load_card`) that imports
  nothing networked. Exit codes match `smoke` (0 ok/warn, 1 on any FAIL, 2 on
  a bad `--backend`). `ready` inherits doctor's offline-purity contract: the
  ≤4s `update` fetch stays the ONLY networked line and degrades quietly, so a
  verdict is always reachable with the link down. Every `Check.fix` is a
  RUNNABLE command wherever one exists — a dead-end warning during an outage
  is the failure this exists to prevent. There is deliberately NO scheduled,
  cron, launchd, or alerting counterpart: user decision, do not add one.

- LAYERING is one-directional and TESTED (`tests/test_layering.py`,
  2026-08-04). `chat/` is the top tier and may reach down; nothing below it
  imports `luxe.chat.*` at module level (`cli` is the one sanctioned
  exception — it is the composition root and sits above chat). `agents/`
  never imports `chat/` at all. Code that two tiers genuinely share lives in
  a NEUTRAL root-tier module: `luxe/mounts.py` (the `mount(8)` parse, shared
  by `chat.origin` and `modelstore`), `luxe/cancel.py`
  (`CancelToken`/`ChatCancelled`/`raise_if_cancelled`, shared by chat and
  gitkit), `luxe/textfmt.py` (`truncate_for_display`, same), `luxe/gitcmd.py`
  (the git subprocess runner). The upper tier re-exports the name so existing
  imports and monkeypatch targets are unaffected
- `luxe/gitcmd.py` is the only place that spawns `git`, EXCEPT the
  benchmark-path call sites that construct argv deliberately (`pr.py`,
  `citations.py`, `spec_validator.py`, `gitclone.py`, `repo_index.py`,
  `chat/smoke.py`) — those stay raw, exact flags matter. `run()` is `git -C
  <repo>` (a bad path is a non-zero exit), `run_in()` is `cwd=<repo>` (a bad
  path RAISES, indistinguishable from a missing git binary); the two are not
  interchangeable and each caller keeps the form it had. Error POLICY stays at
  the call site: the wordings differ per subsystem and are user-visible
- Every git invocation pins the host config that would corrupt CAPTURED output
  (2026-08-12): `-c color.ui=false -c core.pager=cat --no-pager` in
  `gitcmd._CONFIG_PINS`, plus `errors="replace"` decoding. All of it is git's
  own non-tty default, so a default-config host is byte-identical — but
  `color.ui=always` in one user's `~/.gitconfig` puts ANSI escapes into text
  that eight modules PARSE. The two call sites whose output is parsed for
  GRADING (`pr.diff_against_base`, `spec_validator._added_lines_from_diff`)
  pin more: `gitcmd.DIFF_PARSE_PINS` adds `diff.noprefix=false` +
  `diff.mnemonicprefix=false`, the argv carries `--no-ext-diff`, and
  `gitcmd.parse_env()` adds `GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1` + `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`
  (both live in the neutral module, so the two parsers cannot drift).
  Each of those settings rewrites the `+++ b/<path>` header the two parsers
  key on, so a host preference would zero a grade silently rather than fail
  (`tests/test_git_config_pinning.py`)
- A git FAILURE is never returned as an empty diff (2026-08-12).
  `pr.diff_against_base` raises `GitDiffError` (it used to return `(0, 0, "")`
  — the exact value a clean tree gives) and
  `spec_validator._added_lines_from_diff` returns `None` (it used to return
  `[]`). Callers must branch: `maintain_pipeline` records a `diff_stat_failed`
  event and SKIPS both reprompt gates rather than firing a second pass on a
  fabricated 0-additions; `ValidationResult.error` (None on every healthy run)
  marks the diff predicates NOT EVALUATED instead of unmet; `gitkit.apply`
  marks the step failed instead of "no changes produced". Network git/gh calls
  in `pr.py` go through `_run_net` — `gitclone.clone_env()` + a 120s deadline
  that surfaces as `rc=124` with a message, because a credential prompt on an
  inherited stdin has no deadline at all
- The `luxe maintain` PIPELINE lives in `luxe/maintain.py`
  (`maintain_pipeline`), not in `cli.py`; `cli.maintain` is a Click shell that
  forwards its eleven parameters unchanged. Both benchmark adapters spawn
  `python -m luxe.cli maintain`, so the command name, arguments, and option
  surface are frozen — check `luxe maintain --help` against the previous
  revision before shipping a change there. `maintain.py` imports `cli` only
  INSIDE the function (`_resolve_repo`, `_infer_task_type`); `cli` imports it
  at module level
- `~/.luxe` is spelled ONCE, in `luxe/paths.py` (`luxe_home()`), and resolved
  per call so a test that moves `$HOME` is obeyed. Deliberately NOT
  configurable — every one of those paths is also written down in OUTAGE.md.
  `--ephemeral` therefore SUPPRESSES writes at each site (`luxe.ephemeral`,
  chat.sdd) rather than relocating the root: redirecting `luxe_home()` would
  capture the READ paths too (secrets.env, the theme preference), and both
  `secrets.SECRETS_PATH` and `theme._PREF_PATH` bind it at import time, so a
  redirect would be honoured or ignored depending on import order

## Must not
- No cloud provider backends on the BENCHMARK/MAINTAIN path — that path reads
  `omlx_base_url` and talks to MLX/oMLX endpoints only. Reproducibility is the
  reason: a metered, versionless, remotely-updated endpoint cannot anchor a
  bake-off. `luxe chat`/`luxe code` gain ONE sanctioned, opt-in, billable
  exception (2026-08-17): a `backends:` entry with `engine: openrouter`,
  selected per session with `--backend`/`/backend`. It is never a slot default,
  never a host-manifest main/fallback, never a bench or `luxe smoke`/`luxe ready`
  target, and it carries a hard `budget_usd` spend cap the session refuses to
  cross. Do not add a second cloud engine on this precedent
- Break a layering cycle with a FUNCTION-LOCAL import. It hides the cycle from
  the reader and from every static tool, and it is exactly how the three
  cycles fixed on 2026-08-04 survived for months. Move the shared piece down
  to a neutral module instead
- Read `origin/<branch>` in offline-cache repos (stale-ref trap; use local ref or sidecar regrade)
- Use MoE Instruct-2507 model family (long-context fabrication, skips optional tool calls)
- Use `--no-verify`, `--no-gpg-sign`, or other hook-skip flags
- Force-push to `main` or `master`
- Configure dense >30B mxfp8 models (don't fit on 64GB Mac under load)
- Walk a user-chosen root with `Path.rglob`/`Path.glob` — pathlib swallows only
  `PermissionError`, so an unreachable network-backed dir (`OSError(ETIMEDOUT)`)
  crashes the caller. Use `luxe.fswalk.iter_files` (2026-07-29 chat crash)
- Hand-roll the index walkers' prune loop. `luxe.fswalk.iter_pruned` (and
  `iter_pruned_files`, which adds the shared accept-then-size filter) is the
  one traversal for bm25 search, symbol extraction, the repo summary, and
  gitkit's two enumerators. Semantics (fixed 2026-08-05, evidence-gated):
  `excludes` always wins; `keep` only rescues dot-directories that are not
  excluded. The original five call sites' precedence quirk (a `keep` dir
  surviving its own exclusion) was closed after a sweep showed no caller
  can reach the divergent input; `tests/test_fswalk.py` pins the intended
  semantics and documents the one legacy divergence
- Promote configs from `configs/_archive/` (reference-only; not the champion)

## Owns
- src/luxe/**

## Forbids
- src/swarm/**
- src/micro/**
- src/phased/**
